54:{"timestamp":"2026-07-29T08:12:50.904Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"I’m choosing two explicit `for` loops. That keeps all iteration in the supplied fixed semantics, avoids a comprehension’s extra closure/heap machinery, and makes the two loop invariants directly state “accumulator plus the fold over the remaining suffix.” I’m also defining the empty-list case as `0.0`, so the implementation and theorem cover every finite float list instead of silently excluding size zero.","phase":"commentary","memory_citation":null}}
56:{"timestamp":"2026-07-29T08:12:56.842Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0f9d3448af182067016a69b602e1408194bd7a3038f6991ca3","status":"completed","call_id":"call_hFoAvoxp8QoUR85mm19PDMiJ","name":"apply_patch","input":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /workspace/solution.py\n+from typing import List\n+\n+\n+def mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n+    count = len(numbers)\n+    if count == 0:\n+        return 0.0\n+\n+    total = 0.0\n+    for number in numbers:\n+        total = total + number\n+\n+    mean = total / count\n+    deviation = 0.0\n+    for number in numbers:\n+        deviation = deviation + abs(number - mean)\n+\n+    return deviation / count\n*** Add File: /workspace/smoke.py\n+from typing import List\n+\n+\n+def mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n+    count = len(numbers)\n+    if count == 0:\n+        return 0.0\n+\n+    total = 0.0\n+    for number in numbers:\n+        total = total + number\n+\n+    mean = total / count\n+    deviation = 0.0\n+    for number in numbers:\n+        deviation = deviation + abs(number - mean)\n+\n+    return deviation / count\n+\n+\n+assert mean_absolute_deviation([]) == 0.0\n+assert mean_absolute_deviation([1.0]) == 0.0\n+assert mean_absolute_deviation([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]) == 1.0\n*** End Patch\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019facec-d201-7220-b190-f51d20f51425"}}}
